WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.417
A trigonometric equation asks which angles produce a specified coordinate

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:12.451
On one full turn, the height one half occurs twice: once in the first quadrant and once in the second

00:00:12.451 --> 00:00:16.067
Reading only the first crossing would miss another solution.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:25.693
Within the interval from zero inclusive to two pi exclusive, the solutions are pi over six and five pi over six

00:00:25.693 --> 00:00:32.433
The second angle shares the reference angle but lies in the second quadrant, where sine remains positive.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:38.111
Without an interval restriction, full turns produce infinitely many solutions

00:00:38.111 --> 00:00:42.925
Add any integer multiple of two pi to each base angle

00:00:42.925 --> 00:00:50.367
The integer may be positive, zero, or negative, because turning backward also returns to the same coordinates.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:55.826
Factoring a trigonometric equation can reveal several solution families

00:00:55.826 --> 00:01:00.910
This product vanishes when sine is zero or cosine is zero

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:08.767
Dividing by one factor without checking when it vanishes would erase an entire family of valid solutions.
